✅ 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 𝐈𝐧𝐭𝐞𝐫𝐯𝐢𝐞𝐰 𝐒𝐜𝐞𝐧𝐚𝐫𝐢𝐨 #5 – 𝐋𝐨𝐨𝐩 𝐎𝐯𝐞𝐫 𝐔𝐬𝐞𝐫𝐬 𝐢𝐧 𝐀𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐛𝐥𝐞 (𝐒𝐢𝐦𝐩𝐥𝐞 & 𝐀𝐝𝐯𝐚𝐧𝐜𝐞𝐝)

🧠 𝐐𝐮𝐞𝐬𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧:

How do you efficiently create multiple users in Ansible — both with basic names and more detailed attributes like shell preferences?

💡 𝐑𝐞𝐚𝐥-𝐖𝐨𝐫𝐥𝐝 𝐔𝐬𝐞 𝐂𝐚𝐬𝐞:

User provisioning is a common DevOps task. Whether you're onboarding developers, automating server setup, or syncing users across environments, hardcoding them one by one isn’t scalable.

𝐓𝐡𝐢𝐧𝐤 𝐨𝐟 𝐢𝐭 𝐥𝐢𝐤𝐞 𝐜𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐭𝐢𝐧𝐠 𝐚𝐜𝐜𝐨𝐮𝐧𝐭𝐬 𝐚𝐭 𝐚 𝐜𝐨𝐦𝐩𝐚𝐧𝐲:

📋 Some users just need access.

⚙️ Others need special configs like /𝒃𝒊𝒏/𝒛𝒔𝒉 or group membership.

Ansible can handle both cases with simple and structured loops.

💡 𝐏𝐫𝐨 𝐓𝐢𝐩:

You can expand dictionary-based loops to include 𝒈𝒓𝒐𝒖𝒑𝒔, 𝑼𝑰𝑫, 𝒑𝒂𝒔𝒔𝒘𝒐𝒓𝒅, 𝒐𝒓 𝒆𝒗𝒆𝒏 𝒉𝒐𝒎𝒆 𝒅𝒊𝒓𝒆𝒄𝒕𝒐𝒓𝒊𝒆𝒔. Perfect for managing complex user needs in enterprise environments.

📣 𝐁𝐨𝐧𝐮𝐬 𝐔𝐬𝐞 𝐂𝐚𝐬𝐞𝐬:

✅ Package installation

✅ Service management

✅ Firewall rules

✅ Anything with repeatable parameters


=======================


#loop-over-users.yaml 
#Solution: Use loop with both basic and dictionary-style data
---
- name: Scenario #5 - Loop Over Users (Simple and Complex)
  hosts: all
  become: yes
  vars:
    simple_users:
      - dev1
      - dev2
      - dev3

    complex_users:
      - { name: "dev1", shell: "/bin/bash" }
      - { name: "dev2", shell: "/bin/zsh" }
  tasks:

  - name: Create users from a simple list
    user:
      name: "{{ item }}"
      state: present
    loop: "{{ simple_users }}"
  - name: Create users with custom shells from a dictionary
    user:
      name: "{{item.name }}" 
      shell: "{{item.shell }}" 
      state: present
    loop: "{{ complex_users }}"
